1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,728
Nessie already measures skills.

2
00:00:01,728 --> 00:00:05,123
Its public changelog shows success analytics per skill.

3
00:00:05,123 --> 00:00:12,611
Its Skill Evals docs give every run a verdict: Success, Skill error, Unexpected behavior, Other error, or Unknown.

4
00:00:12,611 --> 00:00:18,954
What the public docs don't describe is the next step: turning those failures into fixes a person can review.

5
00:00:18,954 --> 00:00:21,488
Skill Fix Loop is a prototype of that step.

6
00:00:21,488 --> 00:00:25,977
It runs locally, on synthetic data, and it isn't affiliated with Nessie.

7
00:00:26,577 --> 00:00:27,636
Here's the shape.

8
00:00:27,636 --> 00:00:29,599
Traces come in as JSON lines.

9
00:00:29,599 --> 00:00:31,523
Rules give every run a verdict.

10
00:00:31,523 --> 00:00:36,668
Failures are grouped into causes, and each cause gets one drafted edit to the skill file.

11
00:00:36,668 --> 00:00:38,609
All of that runs once, at startup.

12
00:00:38,609 --> 00:00:45,422
Then a person approves or rejects each edit, and replay reports success before and after, on held-out traces.

13
00:00:45,422 --> 00:00:51,533
An LLM can help in just two places: judging runs the rules leave as Unknown, and wording the edit.

14
00:00:51,533 --> 00:00:54,170
Both stay off unless a model is configured.

15
00:00:54,769 --> 00:00:57,659
Let's follow one run, trace dep-086.

16
00:00:57,659 --> 00:01:03,230
The agent checks the branch, installs, builds, then runs vercel deploy with --preview.

17
00:01:03,230 --> 00:01:06,944
That exits with code two, because the newer tool removed the flag.

18
00:01:06,944 --> 00:01:08,843
The agent still reports success.

19
00:01:08,843 --> 00:01:11,938
Verdict rules run in order, and the first match wins.

20
00:01:11,938 --> 00:01:14,007
Rule four looks at the final tool call.

21
00:01:14,007 --> 00:01:19,848
A non-zero exit is a Skill error, whatever the agent said, unless the error looks environmental.

22
00:01:19,848 --> 00:01:23,583
The verdict records the rule, a reason, and the step where it fired.

23
00:01:24,183 --> 00:01:25,339
Next, causes.

24
00:01:25,339 --> 00:01:27,168
Each error line is normalized.

25
00:01:27,168 --> 00:01:33,003
Paths, IDs and numbers are stripped, so runs that differ only in details share one signature.

26
00:01:33,003 --> 00:01:41,769
Signatures become TF-IDF vectors, and agglomerative clustering groups them, one skill at a time, using only failures from the mining split.

27
00:01:41,769 --> 00:01:50,960
For deploy-preview, that gives three causes: a missing Vercel token in twenty-two runs, the removed flag in eighteen, and deploying from main in seven.

28
00:01:51,560 --> 00:01:52,609
Now the fix.

29
00:01:52,609 --> 00:01:56,663
The default drafter is a set of templates matched against the error text.

30
00:01:56,663 --> 00:02:01,241
This one recognizes a renamed flag, so it rewrites step four of the skill file.

31
00:02:01,241 --> 00:02:05,781
You get a one-line diff, and a rationale citing five trace IDs you can click.

32
00:02:05,781 --> 00:02:08,293
A reviewer approves, edits, or rejects.

33
00:02:08,293 --> 00:02:13,864
Each decision is appended to reviews.jsonl with a timestamp, and the latest one wins.

34
00:02:13,864 --> 00:02:16,079
Replay then checks the held-out traces.

35
00:02:16,079 --> 00:02:22,004
Approve only this edit, and deploy-preview goes from forty to forty-four successes out of sixty.

36
00:02:22,004 --> 00:02:24,553
Approve all three, and it reaches fifty-seven.

37
00:02:25,153 --> 00:02:26,846
To run it, type make demo.

38
00:02:26,846 --> 00:02:32,287
It installs the pinned requirements and starts the app on port eight thousand, with no API key.

39
00:02:32,287 --> 00:02:37,704
Five pages follow the loop: skills, skill detail, proposed edit, trace viewer, and results.

40
00:02:37,704 --> 00:02:41,240
For the LLM path, set a model in a .env file.

41
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:44,306
Some things to know before you trust the numbers.

42
00:02:44,306 --> 00:02:46,680
Agent feedback never decides on its own.

43
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:51,338
A clean run where the agent reports failure stays Unknown, under Needs triage.

44
00:02:51,338 --> 00:02:52,846
Replay is simulated.

45
00:02:52,846 --> 00:02:59,412
A held-out failure counts as fixed if an approved diff adds that cause's fix marker, which overstates real gains.

46
00:02:59,412 --> 00:03:06,224
The data is synthetic and easy, so a hundred percent cluster purity proves the plumbing, not real-world accuracy.

47
00:03:06,224 --> 00:03:09,206
Approved diffs are never written back to the skill file.

48
00:03:09,206 --> 00:03:15,492
And repeated workflows, like pg_dump, pg_dump, diff, are flagged as new skill candidates, not fixed.

49
00:03:16,092 --> 00:03:17,046
To recap.

50
00:03:17,046 --> 00:03:18,739
Every verdict names its rule.

51
00:03:18,739 --> 00:03:20,872
Failures become a few named causes.

52
00:03:20,872 --> 00:03:23,825
Each cause gets one diff, backed by trace IDs.

53
00:03:23,825 --> 00:03:26,838
A person decides, and every decision is logged.

54
00:03:26,838 --> 00:03:29,299
And success is measured on held-out runs.

55
00:03:29,299 --> 00:03:40,394
Next steps: real agent reruns instead of simulated replay, checking the judge against human labels, normalizers for each harness, and deciding who may approve edits to shared skills.

56
00:03:40,394 --> 00:03:41,211
Thank you.
